00:12:00.487 --> 00:12:01.920
my mission is I'd kind of

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:04.450
do it probably, initially,
every two weeks.

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:07.872
And it's no more than five
or six, seven minutes.

00:12:07.872 --> 00:12:10.425
So, it's very easy to consume.

00:12:10.425 --> 00:12:12.564
And we'll probably have to make sure,

00:12:12.564 --> 00:12:14.340
when we have a special moment Evan,

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:16.643
we capture it on the vlog.

00:12:16.643 --> 00:12:18.015
- Yeah, I'm excited.

00:12:18.015 --> 00:12:19.832
And for those of you guys watching,

00:12:19.832 --> 00:12:23.318
I've been talking to Stephen
about video for awhile.

00:12:23.318 --> 00:12:26.071
I remember way back on
one of our first hangouts,

00:12:26.071 --> 00:12:27.627
one of our audience
members couldn't make it

00:12:27.627 --> 00:12:30.327
because they were doing cancer treatment,

00:12:30.327 --> 00:12:32.724
and Stephen ended up at the airport

00:12:32.724 --> 00:12:35.028
making a quick little video on his phone

00:12:35.028 --> 00:12:36.531
wishing that person good luck,

00:12:36.531 --> 00:12:37.942
and hope the treatment goes well,

00:12:37.942 --> 00:12:40.422
and we'll see you hopefully
at the next episode.

00:12:40.422 --> 00:12:42.315
And I thought it was a
really cool thing to do for,

00:12:42.315 --> 00:12:43.927
you know, you've got this CEO,

00:12:43.927 --> 00:12:47.168
who's got 13, 14, 15 thousand employees

00:12:47.168 --> 00:12:48.390
and taking out time out of his day

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:50.540
to make a custom video.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.395
And so I think he could do
really well on the vlog,

00:12:53.395 --> 00:12:56.058
and I'm excited that that's coming out.

00:12:56.058 --> 00:12:57.447
Okay, let's get to some questions.

00:12:57.447 --> 00:13:00.571
They've been piling up
as we've been talking.

00:13:00.571 --> 00:13:02.670
The first is from Sharique who's asking,

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:05.215
"How do you surround
yourself with great people

00:13:05.215 --> 00:13:07.798
"who care about the customers?"

00:13:09.177 --> 00:13:10.274
- That's a great question.

00:13:10.274 --> 00:13:11.435
Do you know what one of the things

00:13:11.435 --> 00:13:13.144
you can do in the early days?

00:13:13.144 --> 00:13:16.727
Is probably hiring your
company a customer.

00:13:18.015 --> 00:13:20.496
Or someone who lives,
and walks, and breathes

00:13:20.496 --> 00:13:23.638
the customer journey everyday.

00:13:23.638 --> 00:13:26.662
And the other things we kind of do,

00:13:26.662 --> 00:13:28.079
which I think's pretty cool,

00:13:28.079 --> 00:13:30.808
is have things like
entrepreneurs in residence.

00:13:30.808 --> 00:13:33.405
So, we have Nicole Harding,

00:13:33.405 --> 00:13:36.369
who's come into Sage to work for us.

00:13:36.369 --> 00:13:39.048
And they're entrepreneurs,
they're grafters,

00:13:39.048 --> 00:13:41.898
they started their business and
they've walked in the shoes,

00:13:41.898 --> 00:13:44.232
and they've done maybe
three or four businesses

00:13:44.232 --> 00:13:47.701
where they've done start
ups and grown 'em up,

00:13:47.701 --> 00:13:49.918
have done an exit, gone
on to the next one.

00:13:49.918 --> 00:13:53.757
So, I think the other
thing is, in reality,

00:13:53.757 --> 00:13:56.747
loads of things that we
sort of touched on is

00:13:56.747 --> 00:14:00.334
make sure you as the
entrepreneur and the founder

00:14:00.334 --> 00:14:04.595
talk always about customers,
and you will very much,

00:14:04.595 --> 00:14:07.822
by that, you will attract
similar individuals

00:14:07.822 --> 00:14:10.522
who really care passionately
about customers.

00:14:10.522 --> 00:14:13.441
And then the other thing I
think is a really good idea,

00:14:13.441 --> 00:14:16.345
if it's achievable, is you
actually join the communities

00:14:16.345 --> 00:14:19.825
of your customers, and you
can do that on social media.

00:14:19.825 --> 00:14:24.650
But also I'd always
advocate, if you can without

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:26.980
crossing the line in terms
of upsetting customers,

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:29.401
if you can recruit customers
into your business,

00:14:29.401 --> 00:14:31.877
you'll understand, obviously
the customer perspective,

00:14:31.877 --> 00:14:33.170
but also understand how to scale

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:34.796
and grow your business as well.

00:14:34.796 --> 00:14:38.146
That's a fantastic lens for you
to have within your company.

00:14:38.146 --> 00:14:38.979
- That's interesting.

00:14:38.979 --> 00:14:42.261
I'm curious what you
guys do with customers.

00:14:42.261 --> 00:14:44.692
So you have some of them who
sit in on board meetings,

00:14:44.692 --> 00:14:46.532
or who advise you guys,
or have you hired them

00:14:46.532 --> 00:14:48.212
away from their business
and now they're working

00:14:48.212 --> 00:14:50.839
in side stage on different projects?

00:14:50.839 --> 00:14:52.070
- Yeah, we've got a number of things.

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:53.954
Actually, I think you
met some of these guys.

00:14:53.954 --> 00:14:56.209
We got this Sage Ambassadors program,

00:14:56.209 --> 00:14:59.488
so we've got business experts.

00:14:59.488 --> 00:15:01.959
And I go out all by myself
probably every month

00:15:01.959 --> 00:15:04.904
and meet up country by country,

00:15:04.904 --> 00:15:06.993
and meet the local
management team at Sage,

00:15:06.993 --> 00:15:11.563
talk about issues relevant
to the customer journey.

00:15:11.563 --> 00:15:14.692
And also I tend to check
in with those guys.

00:15:14.692 --> 00:15:17.285
The other things we do
is have these kind of

00:15:17.285 --> 00:15:19.857
conceptive entrepreneurs in residence,

00:15:19.857 --> 00:15:21.839
where they come in and kind of

00:15:21.839 --> 00:15:25.333
help us understand more about
what are the pain points

00:15:25.333 --> 00:15:28.141
and gain points that customers see.

00:15:28.141 --> 00:15:31.509
And then also, yeah, we have taken on

00:15:31.509 --> 00:15:33.069
some folks, I mentioned Nicole.

00:15:33.069 --> 00:15:34.778
There's a few folks, a
lot of folks actually,

00:15:34.778 --> 00:15:37.494
in the company who have been
entrepreneurs themselves,

00:15:37.494 --> 00:15:38.674
and come to work for Sage,

00:15:38.674 --> 00:15:40.801
because there's no better perspective

00:15:40.801 --> 00:15:44.968
than having kind of done
it, and worn the T-shirt,

00:15:45.905 --> 00:15:47.824
and started a couple of companies.

00:15:47.824 --> 00:15:50.112
And also, Evan, not only companies

00:15:50.112 --> 00:15:51.562
that have been successful,

00:15:51.562 --> 00:15:53.414
but some of the best
learnings you get is when

00:15:53.414 --> 00:15:57.327
you hit the skids, and you have a failure.

00:15:57.327 --> 00:15:59.543
And you have to exit prematurely,

00:15:59.543 --> 00:16:01.679
or you don't realize your dreams,

00:16:01.679 --> 00:16:04.515
because, inevitably, the reality is

00:16:04.515 --> 00:16:06.262
there's something fantastic in the U.S.,

00:16:06.262 --> 00:16:09.925
there's millions of
businesses start every year.

00:16:09.925 --> 00:16:11.179
Over in the U.K. there's about

00:16:11.179 --> 00:16:13.664
600,000 new businesses started last year.

00:16:13.664 --> 00:16:15.087
But within five years probably,

00:16:15.087 --> 00:16:17.745
60% of those would have failed.

00:16:17.745 --> 00:16:18.872
I don't like that word failure,

00:16:18.872 --> 00:16:20.486
'cause if you fail and you fail fast,

00:16:20.486 --> 00:16:21.605
that's probably the best learnings

00:16:21.605 --> 00:16:23.569
you're ever going to
get as an entrepreneur.

00:16:23.569 --> 00:16:24.654
And you can go on a greater,

00:16:24.654 --> 00:16:28.375
fantastically successful
business next time.

00:16:28.375 --> 00:16:29.208
- I love it.

00:16:29.208 --> 00:16:31.220
And thinking about
attracting a great team,

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:34.170
I would say too, that when
you stand for something,

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:36.776
it's easy to get the
right people on board.

00:16:36.776 --> 00:16:38.617
If it's really clear in
all of your messaging

00:16:38.617 --> 00:16:41.815
and all your activities that
you care about the customer,

00:16:41.815 --> 00:16:44.033
you're going to attract people
who care about the customers.

00:16:44.033 --> 00:16:45.943
If people are applying to Sage,

00:16:45.943 --> 00:16:48.119
and they see Stephen
on his vlog interacting

00:16:48.119 --> 00:16:50.906
with the customers and
continuing to care about them,

00:16:50.906 --> 00:16:53.450
then the people who like
that are going to be the ones

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:55.587
who also believe in the same thing.

00:16:55.587 --> 00:16:57.193
And so if you're really
clear about your mission,

00:16:57.193 --> 00:16:59.244
what you stand for,
what you're here to do,

00:16:59.244 --> 00:17:02.443
and if serving customer
needs is your mission,

00:17:02.443 --> 00:17:03.789
then you're going to attract people

00:17:03.789 --> 00:17:05.611
who want to be a part of that.

00:17:05.611 --> 00:17:07.654
If it's unclear what you stand for,

00:17:07.654 --> 00:17:09.693
then if you're hiring a programmer,

00:17:09.693 --> 00:17:11.347
or customer service representative,

00:17:11.347 --> 00:17:13.809
or whoever into your business,

00:17:13.809 --> 00:17:16.677
you're going to get all
kinds of different people.

00:17:16.677 --> 00:17:17.983
And so you want to be
really upfront and clear

00:17:17.983 --> 00:17:20.263
what your mission is so
attract like-minded people,

00:17:20.263 --> 00:17:21.544
and the people who don't
agree with your mission,

00:17:21.544 --> 00:17:23.533
they're never going to apply.

00:17:23.533 --> 00:17:26.948
So, they self-eliminate from the start.

00:17:26.948 --> 00:17:28.244
Great, let's get to some more.

00:17:28.244 --> 00:17:31.361
So, Dash is writing in,
"How would you suggest

00:17:31.361 --> 00:17:33.872
"being successful with limited resources?

00:17:33.872 --> 00:17:36.661
"Do you believe in your
character, personality, and

00:17:36.661 --> 00:17:38.420
"or values driving the business

00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:41.549
"as opposed to the equipment you own?"

00:17:41.549 --> 00:17:43.192
- Yeah, Dash, that's a great question,

00:17:43.192 --> 00:17:45.404
and I think we'd probably also say

00:17:45.404 --> 00:17:48.026
it's all about leadership,
it's your personality.

00:17:48.026 --> 00:17:50.943
You are the expression of the idea.

00:17:52.021 --> 00:17:53.517
All the product you sell.

00:17:53.517 --> 00:17:57.098
And we talk about the integrity
that you have, the love,

00:17:57.098 --> 00:18:00.400
the dream, and the
inspiration and the vision

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:03.234
that you can not only
inspire your customers,

00:18:03.234 --> 00:18:05.568
maybe partners, but also people who work

00:18:05.568 --> 00:18:08.871
for you in the business colleagues.

00:18:08.871 --> 00:18:10.612
That's really, really fundamental.

00:18:10.612 --> 00:18:13.323
And the personality is really important.

00:18:13.323 --> 00:18:16.754
So, I think there's a lot
of things you as a leader,

00:18:16.754 --> 00:18:19.257
when you want to stand out, it's so easy

00:18:19.257 --> 00:18:21.297
to be swayed by a lot of people.

00:18:21.297 --> 00:18:23.776
And I think if you have a
really strong conviction,

00:18:23.776 --> 00:18:26.732
and to Evan's point, hashtag believe,

00:18:26.732 --> 00:18:29.571
then how do you stand out from the crowd?

00:18:29.571 --> 00:18:31.638
We stand in the shoes of the customer.

00:18:31.638 --> 00:18:32.680
You've got the big ideas,

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:35.596
so I think there's a whole theme around,

00:18:35.596 --> 00:18:37.280
obviously you want to
listen to the colleagues,

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:40.194
you want to listen to the
market really carefully,

00:18:40.194 --> 00:18:41.651
but you also got to be confident

00:18:41.651 --> 00:18:44.106
and be decisive and make those calls.

00:18:44.106 --> 00:18:47.616
And then put a lot of the
word behind the few hours

00:18:47.616 --> 00:18:50.326
and really focus on being successful.

00:18:50.326 --> 00:18:54.831
And I'd always encourage you
to have that beginners mind.

00:18:54.831 --> 00:18:56.353
Be different, think differently,

00:18:56.353 --> 00:18:57.717
think about the next thing,

00:18:57.717 --> 00:19:02.065
what is it, especially in the
word of disintermediation,

00:19:02.065 --> 00:19:04.260
customers will, in five years time,

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:06.931
be doing that you can invent today.

00:19:06.931 --> 00:19:09.388
So, do think differently,
and I think a lot

00:19:09.388 --> 00:19:11.646
of that comes from, as you said,

00:19:11.646 --> 00:19:14.537
it's your personality, and make your brand

00:19:14.537 --> 00:19:17.121
very visible in terms of your personality

00:19:17.121 --> 00:19:19.075
and your leadership,
'cause that will inspire

00:19:19.075 --> 00:19:21.794
all the important people
like the customers

00:19:21.794 --> 00:19:23.152
and especially your colleagues.

00:19:23.152 --> 00:19:26.238
If you get those two right,
you've got a fantastic business.

00:19:26.238 --> 00:19:27.726
- What was the analogy you used?

00:19:27.726 --> 00:19:30.226
Focus on the wood behind your few arrows?

00:19:30.226 --> 00:19:31.533
Is that what you said?

00:19:31.533 --> 00:19:32.541
- Oh. Yeah, yeah.

00:19:32.541 --> 00:19:35.060
So, this is kind of, this is crazy.

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:39.103
This is not a metaphor
for the digital age.

00:19:39.103 --> 00:19:42.068
But when you're an entrepreneur,

00:19:42.068 --> 00:19:44.853
one of the biggest problems
you have is prioritization.

00:19:44.853 --> 00:19:46.122
Hey, this is really interesting,

00:19:46.122 --> 00:19:50.289
apparently prioritize, I think
it comes from a Greek word.

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:52.423
And you know how we say,

00:19:52.423 --> 00:19:54.062
"What are your top four priorities?"

00:19:54.062 --> 00:19:56.957
Well, that actually breaches
the essence of the Greek word.

00:19:56.957 --> 00:20:01.062
Prioritize means you
can only do one thing.

00:20:01.062 --> 00:20:02.456
Uno. One thing.

00:20:02.456 --> 00:20:03.289
No more.

00:20:03.289 --> 00:20:04.854
You can't do two priorities,
three priorities.

00:20:04.854 --> 00:20:06.677
So it's ruthless, and obviously,

00:20:06.677 --> 00:20:09.294
in a modern age, we
have lots of priorities.

00:20:09.294 --> 00:20:11.692
The biggest challenge
we have as entrepreneurs

00:20:11.692 --> 00:20:14.061
is that single focus around the thing

00:20:14.061 --> 00:20:16.975
that's really going to move
the needle, move the dart.

00:20:16.975 --> 00:20:20.638
And so we have this phrase
as, you can have 50 arrows

00:20:20.638 --> 00:20:23.316
out there, and go to 50 markets,

00:20:23.316 --> 00:20:25.728
and try and do 50 different things,

00:20:25.728 --> 00:20:29.576
or generate and create
14 different products.

00:20:29.576 --> 00:20:31.565
We'd say, no, no, no.

00:20:31.565 --> 00:20:34.900
Have a big vision, but then day to day

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:38.359
for the first 90 days, put
all the wood behind one arrow.

00:20:38.359 --> 00:20:39.542
One arrow, as in

00:20:39.542 --> 00:20:40.375
(whooshing sound)

00:20:40.375 --> 00:20:42.156
bows and arrows and
all that sort of stuff.

00:20:42.156 --> 00:20:42.989
- Yeah, yeah.

00:20:42.989 --> 00:20:44.478
- And get all the wood on those arrows.

00:20:44.478 --> 00:20:46.784
So you put approach huge critical mass.

00:20:46.784 --> 00:20:50.499
And they create a beachhead
where you've got momentum,

00:20:50.499 --> 00:20:52.211
you've got fanatical customers,

00:20:52.211 --> 00:20:55.733
and then you use that
love to actually go on

00:20:55.733 --> 00:20:57.772
to the next beachhead, and
then the next beachhead,

00:20:57.772 --> 00:20:59.930
and then the next.

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:02.205
Honestly, my experience of entrepreneurs,

00:21:02.205 --> 00:21:05.149
we are so optimistic, we are so excited,

00:21:05.149 --> 00:21:07.235
we are so into our idea.

00:21:07.235 --> 00:21:10.114
And yet sometimes we've
just got to check ourselves,

00:21:10.114 --> 00:21:13.266
and instead trying to be
all things to all people,

00:21:13.266 --> 00:21:15.849
we should be brilliant
at one thing at a time.

00:21:15.849 --> 00:21:19.336
And then that can build a great company.

00:21:19.336 --> 00:21:20.169
- I like it.

00:21:20.169 --> 00:21:22.444
I've never head that expression
before, so I like it.

00:21:22.444 --> 00:21:23.586
It's good, it's good.

00:21:23.586 --> 00:21:26.664
Extra ammunition to play with there.

00:21:26.664 --> 00:21:27.772
To the question of limited resources,

00:21:27.772 --> 00:21:29.511
I mean, that's entrepreneurship.

00:21:29.511 --> 00:21:31.974
You never have all the
resources that you need.

00:21:31.974 --> 00:21:34.570
That's why you go out
and create something new

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:36.711
and innovative, because you're
forced up against a wall

00:21:36.711 --> 00:21:38.732
to find another way to stand.

00:21:38.732 --> 00:21:40.561
I think what really helped me

00:21:40.561 --> 00:21:42.989
when I was first getting
started was recognizing that

00:21:42.989 --> 00:21:44.980
people that I looked up to,
the people that I aspired

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:47.146
to have that kind of impact,

00:21:47.146 --> 00:21:49.975
some of the people behind me on the wall,

00:21:49.975 --> 00:21:52.883
they started with less than
what I already have right now.

00:21:52.883 --> 00:21:55.889
The people who you look up
to, and you want to be like,

00:21:55.889 --> 00:21:58.093
the titans in your industry,
look at their story,

00:21:58.093 --> 00:21:59.835
and look at how they started.

00:21:59.835 --> 00:22:02.083
Chances are it wasn't just handed to them,

00:22:02.083 --> 00:22:03.494
and they started off with less

00:22:03.494 --> 00:22:05.704
than what you already have right now.

00:22:05.704 --> 00:22:08.223
And so I use that as motivation to say,

00:22:08.223 --> 00:22:11.081
these guys could do it, I can do it too.

00:22:11.081 --> 00:22:12.763
And instead of seeing limited resources

00:22:12.763 --> 00:22:17.344
as a complaining point, use
that as your opportunity.

00:22:17.344 --> 00:22:19.016
That's where you have a chance

00:22:19.016 --> 00:22:20.762
to do something different and stand out.

00:22:20.762 --> 00:22:22.691
That's the game. That's entrepreneurship.

00:22:22.691 --> 00:22:23.942
You will have limited resources.

00:22:23.942 --> 00:22:24.775
- I love it.

00:22:24.775 --> 00:22:26.017
- You got to use them wisely.

00:22:26.017 --> 00:22:27.379
Put the wood behind the few arrows.

00:22:27.379 --> 00:22:28.496
- I love it.

00:22:28.496 --> 00:22:29.355
(Evan laughing)

00:22:29.355 --> 00:22:31.890
Hey, you're going to love this,
Evan, you're going to love this.

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:34.457
So, one thing I talk a lot about here is,

00:22:34.457 --> 00:22:37.723
and you'll love this,
you can have this quote,

00:22:37.723 --> 00:22:40.709
"The answer is never more resources.

00:22:40.709 --> 00:22:44.376
"The answer is always
more resourcefulness."

00:22:46.694 --> 00:22:49.214
And that's why entrepreneurs
are hugely successful.

00:22:49.214 --> 00:22:51.308
They always overcome difficulties.

00:22:51.308 --> 00:22:53.405
There are never any excuses.

00:22:53.405 --> 00:22:55.738
They always touch the stars.

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:58.310
I think we got to save
that quote for next time,

00:22:58.310 --> 00:22:59.181
you'll lead us off with it.

00:22:59.181 --> 00:23:00.621
That's a good one.

00:23:00.621 --> 00:23:02.193
Yeah, it reminds me of Steve Wozniak,

00:23:02.193 --> 00:23:04.776
who co-founded Apple, who said,

00:23:05.767 --> 00:23:08.292
"Stop thinking about the
resources that you don't have,

00:23:08.292 --> 00:23:10.064
"and instead think about what you can do

00:23:10.064 --> 00:23:11.613
"with what you do have."

00:23:11.613 --> 00:23:14.625
And I think once you change
your mindset from just being

00:23:14.625 --> 00:23:17.608
complaining, or desperate, or upset,

00:23:17.608 --> 00:23:19.749
to a positive one, okay
I have something here,

00:23:19.749 --> 00:23:21.875
let's use what I have to build it.

00:23:21.875 --> 00:23:24.249
That's when you start
becoming more successful.

00:23:24.249 --> 00:23:25.416
So, I like it.

00:23:26.656 --> 00:23:28.487
Next, Randy is writing in,

00:23:28.487 --> 00:23:30.246
"Hi Stephen, it's Randy.

00:23:30.246 --> 00:23:32.434
"I own a resort on an island.

00:23:32.434 --> 00:23:34.327
"Should I act on all the feedback

00:23:34.327 --> 00:23:38.707
"from my customers, and
should I encourage feedback?"

00:23:38.707 --> 00:23:41.601
- Yeah, I think Randy, if you own a resort

00:23:41.601 --> 00:23:44.851
you need to ping Evan and me an invite.

00:23:46.162 --> 00:23:46.995
We'd love that.

00:23:46.995 --> 00:23:48.206
(laughing)

00:23:48.206 --> 00:23:50.991
So, I think just thinking
of what people like,

00:23:50.991 --> 00:23:52.506
obviously people like Trip Advisor,

00:23:52.506 --> 00:23:54.729
but there's just tons of social sites

00:23:54.729 --> 00:23:57.479
where everything is about reviews

00:23:58.534 --> 00:24:00.816
and obviously, seeing what
people like Airbnb have done,

00:24:00.816 --> 00:24:03.460
and all those other
guys, I think you're in

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:06.793
a super, almost transparent marketplace.

00:24:07.779 --> 00:24:10.106
And that's obviously
fueled by social media.

00:24:10.106 --> 00:24:12.736
And I think, just looking at ConsumerLink,

00:24:12.736 --> 00:24:15.285
the data point is about 70% of people now

00:24:15.285 --> 00:24:18.287
kind of bought resorts and stuff online.

00:24:18.287 --> 00:24:21.174
The old kind of model
going through things like

00:24:21.174 --> 00:24:24.047
travel agents and stuff
is pretty redundant.

00:24:24.047 --> 00:24:27.494
And the really important
thing is this is where,

00:24:27.494 --> 00:24:30.103
and this is so important, customer love.

00:24:30.103 --> 00:24:32.945
How do we inspire customer
love in terms of resort?

00:24:32.945 --> 00:24:36.953
And I think a message for
entrepreneurs I'd always give is,

00:24:36.953 --> 00:24:39.797
if you can just do every little thing,

00:24:39.797 --> 00:24:43.547
every little interaction, like
the book-in, the reservation,

00:24:43.547 --> 00:24:46.630
the welcome, the room, the breakfast,

00:24:47.888 --> 00:24:50.020
whatever it is you do when you break down

00:24:50.020 --> 00:24:52.185
the whole journey for a customer.

00:24:52.185 --> 00:24:54.043
And break it down to every component.

00:24:54.043 --> 00:24:57.630
Every touch point, every
moment of their day.

00:24:57.630 --> 00:25:01.890
If you just exceed their
expectations just by a little bit,

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:04.869
then you will be remembered
forever by that individual.

00:25:04.869 --> 00:25:06.560
And I think the other thing we forget,

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:09.057
it's like a resort vacation,

00:25:09.057 --> 00:25:11.057
is wow, if it goes well,

00:25:12.473 --> 00:25:16.409
typically you don't get
a level of fanaticism.

00:25:16.409 --> 00:25:17.956
If it goes exceptionally well,

00:25:17.956 --> 00:25:19.590
they'll tell maybe five people.

00:25:19.590 --> 00:25:23.923
If it goes badly, they'll
tell a hundred people.

00:25:23.923 --> 00:25:26.602
So, the cons are stacked against you

00:25:26.602 --> 00:25:29.017
on the downside if you get it wrong.

00:25:29.017 --> 00:25:31.088
Now, I would say also that

00:25:31.088 --> 00:25:34.682
I think authenticity is really important.

00:25:34.682 --> 00:25:38.851
I get customers, we got wow,
three million customers,

00:25:38.851 --> 00:25:40.817
businesses around the
world use our technology.

00:25:40.817 --> 00:25:44.283
Do we get it right every day
of the week, every second?

00:25:44.283 --> 00:25:45.729
Absolutely not.

00:25:45.729 --> 00:25:48.614
Do we drop the ball? Yeah.

00:25:48.614 --> 00:25:52.697
And when we do that, I'll
get customers DMing me,

00:25:54.297 --> 00:25:57.797
emailing me, calling me, and I'll say wow,

00:25:58.686 --> 00:26:01.582
my aspiration is that
we have the happiest,

00:26:01.582 --> 00:26:05.001
and most delighted, and raving fanatical,

00:26:05.001 --> 00:26:09.744
incredibly advocates out
there, but we drop the ball.

00:26:09.744 --> 00:26:12.520
Sometimes the product say
doesn't work, or the internet

00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:15.128
it would connect in or the
cloud went down, whatever.

00:26:15.128 --> 00:26:18.388
There's some stuff, just stuff happens.

00:26:18.388 --> 00:26:20.065
So, what I do in those situations,

00:26:20.065 --> 00:26:22.178
and you could really do
this powerfully in a resort,

00:26:22.178 --> 00:26:26.062
when stuff goes wrong, the
visibility of you, Randy,

00:26:26.062 --> 00:26:27.856
as the leader, picking up the phone,

00:26:27.856 --> 00:26:30.353
calling on their cellphone at that moment,

00:26:30.353 --> 00:26:32.936
having a record response
team, sorting it out,

00:26:32.936 --> 00:26:35.610
making them whole, and
you might get a bowl

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:36.892
of fruit delivered to their room

00:26:36.892 --> 00:26:39.249
or whatever it is, is very powerful.

00:26:39.249 --> 00:26:41.100
And then the other thing I'd say is

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:43.762
when they go back, they
come back to say Toronto,

00:26:43.762 --> 00:26:47.777
or Vancouver, or
Minneapolis two weeks later.

00:26:47.777 --> 00:26:50.814
You know how it is on a
vacation, this is interesting.

00:26:50.814 --> 00:26:52.487
When you have a vacation
you have the best time

00:26:52.487 --> 00:26:54.153
in the world, and you talk about dreams

00:26:54.153 --> 00:26:55.198
and all that sort of stuff,

00:26:55.198 --> 00:26:57.402
and you have really sort of
these esoteric conversations

00:26:57.402 --> 00:27:00.171
with your partner or your friends.

00:27:00.171 --> 00:27:03.742
When you get back, within
six hours of getting back,

00:27:03.742 --> 00:27:05.949
you feel like you haven't had a vacation.

00:27:05.949 --> 00:27:07.336
Do you have that feeling?

00:27:07.336 --> 00:27:10.616
Like wow, I've just been
away for two weeks in Hawaii,

00:27:10.616 --> 00:27:13.860
and I'm back here, and
wow, just my emails,

00:27:13.860 --> 00:27:17.522
my inbox, I'm overwhelmed, it's crazy,

00:27:17.522 --> 00:27:20.113
I can't see the wood from the trees.

00:27:20.113 --> 00:27:22.208
And it feel like you haven't
had a vacation at all.

00:27:22.208 --> 00:27:24.623
Now, how powerful would it be, Randy,

00:27:24.623 --> 00:27:28.529
if two weeks after that person's
vacation on your resort,

00:27:28.529 --> 00:27:31.239
when something went wrong,
they got a bouquet of flowers

00:27:31.239 --> 00:27:33.731
to send we're really
sorry, we dropped the ball,

00:27:33.731 --> 00:27:36.413
we hope you had an amazing time.

00:27:36.413 --> 00:27:37.772
'Cause they'd remember you forever,

00:27:37.772 --> 00:27:38.851
and they would be your advocates.

00:27:38.851 --> 00:27:41.630
'Cause it is just about
those little moment of magic

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:43.919
that you create where you give them

00:27:43.919 --> 00:27:46.985
an experience that blows their brain.

00:27:46.985 --> 00:27:47.818
- I love it.

00:27:47.818 --> 00:27:50.377
That extra love. I love it.

00:27:50.377 --> 00:27:52.879
So, Randy is from Trinidad,
the island's on Trinidad,

00:27:52.879 --> 00:27:54.597
and he's writing in
that he's writing all of

00:27:54.597 --> 00:27:57.743
this stuff down, he's loving your advice.

00:27:57.743 --> 00:27:59.418
Randy, I would say I would
split into two things.

00:27:59.418 --> 00:28:03.094
One, there's the algorithmic
marketing side of things

00:28:03.094 --> 00:28:05.271
with your Google Reviews
and your Trip Advisor

00:28:05.271 --> 00:28:07.598
and Expedia reviews, and all the new game

00:28:07.598 --> 00:28:09.873
that you have to play because
when people are booking

00:28:09.873 --> 00:28:11.699
they want to see that you
have positive reviews.

00:28:11.699 --> 00:28:14.938
So yes, incentivizing your
customers to leave reviews

00:28:14.938 --> 00:28:16.578
and share their honest opinions,

00:28:16.578 --> 00:28:19.768
that will help you with new marketing.

00:28:19.768 --> 00:28:22.124
But on the other side is just
providing a great experience.

00:28:22.124 --> 00:28:23.312
Everything Stephen's been talking about.

00:28:23.312 --> 00:28:25.944
I would study Steve Wynn, I would study

00:28:25.944 --> 00:28:29.611
the Four Seasons Hotel,
the classic story of

00:28:30.787 --> 00:28:33.313
somebody left their bags at your hotel,

00:28:33.313 --> 00:28:35.314
and they went to the
airport, and the bell hop

00:28:35.314 --> 00:28:37.859
gets the bags and follows them in the cab,

00:28:37.859 --> 00:28:40.140
and delivers them at the
airport to them, right?

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:41.950
Just looking for extra ways.

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:43.606
If somebody asks for
directions to the pool,

00:28:43.606 --> 00:28:46.322
don't just tell them, take them
to the pool, and show them.

00:28:46.322 --> 00:28:48.418
Every little touch point,
everything Stephen was saying,

00:28:48.418 --> 00:28:50.292
to try to make it a memorable experience,

00:28:50.292 --> 00:28:54.008
to turn that one off I'm
going to go to Trinidad

00:28:54.008 --> 00:28:56.644
and check out this new resort,

00:28:56.644 --> 00:29:01.160
versus every winter I'm going
to this resort in Trinidad,

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:03.155
and I'm bringing my whole family with me

00:29:03.155 --> 00:29:05.988
because these guys get it
and have the best service.

00:29:05.988 --> 00:29:08.467
If you give them that amazing experience,

00:29:08.467 --> 00:29:10.738
they're going to want
to come back every year,

00:29:10.738 --> 00:29:13.396
tell their friends about it as well.

00:29:13.396 --> 00:29:14.825
- Yeah, on that, Evan, I think it's right

00:29:14.825 --> 00:29:17.333
to the subject, how do you stand out?

00:29:17.333 --> 00:29:19.850
I think this is a great area where Randy,

00:29:19.850 --> 00:29:21.885
especially if you're in
that beautiful island,

00:29:21.885 --> 00:29:23.448
Trinidad and Tobago.

00:29:23.448 --> 00:29:25.198
I love the Caribbean.

00:29:26.566 --> 00:29:28.886
You can really sit down and think about,

00:29:28.886 --> 00:29:30.879
based on all the reviews,

00:29:30.879 --> 00:29:33.395
number one is kind of any
entrepreneur would say,

00:29:33.395 --> 00:29:37.915
what market am I going
for, where is the audience?

00:29:37.915 --> 00:29:40.486
And I think this is where
you can be really honest.

00:29:40.486 --> 00:29:44.180
So maybe, I don't know,
maybe your market is

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:46.560
20 to 30 year olds, maybe it's travelers,

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:48.295
maybe it's families.

00:29:48.295 --> 00:29:52.222
So, where these kind of
particularly resorts get it wrong is

00:29:52.222 --> 00:29:54.706
when they start mixing up the audiences.

00:29:54.706 --> 00:29:57.716
So, you get young families,
and then you get party animals

00:29:57.716 --> 00:30:00.677
and 25 year olds going
on spring break parties.

00:30:00.677 --> 00:30:03.231
That's going to be a disaster.

00:30:03.231 --> 00:30:04.384
'Cause no one's going to be happy.

00:30:04.384 --> 00:30:06.068
'Cause the guys that
want to go out all night,

00:30:06.068 --> 00:30:07.928
and drink all night, and party all night,

00:30:07.928 --> 00:30:09.070
will be waking up all the families.

00:30:09.070 --> 00:30:11.774
So, you need to really say,
what is the market I'm serving?

00:30:11.774 --> 00:30:12.979
Who is the segment?

00:30:12.979 --> 00:30:14.137
Is it families?

00:30:14.137 --> 00:30:17.359
Is it what generation, what nationality?

00:30:17.359 --> 00:30:19.628
You know, you can get really granular.

00:30:19.628 --> 00:30:21.978
And then I think people really
appreciate honesty as well,

00:30:21.978 --> 00:30:24.314
so on your website you should say, look,

00:30:24.314 --> 00:30:27.681
if you're a young family, you're
probably not going to have

00:30:27.681 --> 00:30:30.990
a great experience here
'cause this is a party resort.

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:34.293
Yeah, but if you're 18 to 30 years old,

00:30:34.293 --> 00:30:35.988
and you want to have the
best time of your life,

00:30:35.988 --> 00:30:37.520
this is the resort for you.

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:39.494
And then you really meet the market,

00:30:39.494 --> 00:30:43.116
and everything's steelpans
when they get to the resort,

00:30:43.116 --> 00:30:47.620
all the great music that
comes from the Caribbean,

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:50.312
all the fantastic stuff,
cocktails on the beach,

00:30:50.312 --> 00:30:51.742
rum punches, all that sort of stuff's

00:30:51.742 --> 00:30:55.318
where you're very much
differentiating yourself,

00:30:55.318 --> 00:30:58.829
making yourself different
from the kind of big brands.

00:30:58.829 --> 00:31:01.774
So you can create a unique
experience for your customers

00:31:01.774 --> 00:31:04.960
to really meet their needs and wants.

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:07.336
And they go away feeling like

00:31:07.336 --> 00:31:10.350
they've been sitting with
Bob Marley for two weeks

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:13.264
and experiencing the real Caribbean.

00:31:13.264 --> 00:31:15.736
So, I think you can do that,
and then I would advocate

00:31:15.736 --> 00:31:18.099
really to what Evan said even more,

00:31:18.099 --> 00:31:20.932
that you want to get
that social listening.

00:31:20.932 --> 00:31:21.988
And actually on this area,

00:31:21.988 --> 00:31:24.539
there's some brilliant social
listening tools you can get

00:31:24.539 --> 00:31:28.034
where not only do people write reviews,

00:31:28.034 --> 00:31:30.153
but there's stuff you
don't see that they write

00:31:30.153 --> 00:31:31.935
in other places where you can get

00:31:31.935 --> 00:31:33.769
a pulse of what's going on.

00:31:33.769 --> 00:31:35.159
And I'd absolutely say that.

00:31:35.159 --> 00:31:36.551
And if there's stuff out there,

00:31:36.551 --> 00:31:38.277
the best thing you can
do as an entrepreneur

00:31:38.277 --> 00:31:39.715
is know about it yourself.

00:31:39.715 --> 00:31:42.132
The worst thing you can have happen to you

00:31:42.132 --> 00:31:45.323
is for someone to denigrate
your brand, or your product,

00:31:45.323 --> 00:31:47.916
or your resort and not tell you about it.

00:31:47.916 --> 00:31:49.130
If they tell you about it,

00:31:49.130 --> 00:31:52.404
you've got every opportunity to fix it.

00:31:52.404 --> 00:31:55.776
And with these things, you
know this as consumers,

00:31:55.776 --> 00:31:59.297
when you fix stuff for
customers, when they go wrong,

00:31:59.297 --> 00:32:01.117
that's when you see great companies.

00:32:01.117 --> 00:32:04.490
Great companies always will make mistakes,

00:32:04.490 --> 00:32:07.671
but when great companies make
mistakes, they step up tall,

00:32:07.671 --> 00:32:09.648
they stand shoulder to
shoulder with the customer,

00:32:09.648 --> 00:32:12.344
and they fix it and
make the customer whole.

00:32:12.344 --> 00:32:13.177
- I love it.

00:32:13.177 --> 00:32:14.915
Paula and Peter, we're
coming to you guys next,

00:32:14.915 --> 00:32:17.984
but I just have a follow
up question for Stephen.

00:32:17.984 --> 00:32:20.673
I agree with everything you say.

00:32:20.673 --> 00:32:23.129
You're going to have some
people thinking, okay,

00:32:23.129 --> 00:32:27.086
even though I get most my
business from the party crowd,

00:32:27.086 --> 00:32:29.468
I don't want to lose the families,

00:32:29.468 --> 00:32:31.480
because my place isn't full,

00:32:31.480 --> 00:32:34.310
and so I don't want to turn away business.

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:38.197
What do you say, how do you
respond to that reaction?

00:32:38.197 --> 00:32:41.624
- I think the beauty of
a resort-type business,

00:32:41.624 --> 00:32:44.334
the economics is all about utilization.

00:32:44.334 --> 00:32:46.459
And then once you've got the utilization,

00:32:46.459 --> 00:32:48.154
that probably covers your cost.

00:32:48.154 --> 00:32:51.115
And then the profitability you make is

00:32:51.115 --> 00:32:55.752
through a food, beverage,
all those sort of things.

00:32:55.752 --> 00:32:57.528
So, there's a number of options you have

00:32:57.528 --> 00:32:59.851
in a resort business, I
wouldn't intend to be an expert,

00:32:59.851 --> 00:33:02.517
but you can look at times of the year.

00:33:02.517 --> 00:33:06.184
You could have the same
resort in vacations,

00:33:07.832 --> 00:33:11.428
like Christmas vacation, all families.

00:33:11.428 --> 00:33:12.771
And then you could say, right,

00:33:12.771 --> 00:33:16.938
now we're in mid-January,
on to middle of February,

00:33:17.882 --> 00:33:19.829
you could just say it's
going to be party resort,

00:33:19.829 --> 00:33:21.855
and you could actually change the spaces

00:33:21.855 --> 00:33:24.868
and the areas to
accommodate your audience.

00:33:24.868 --> 00:33:27.644
What I would say is you
kind of don't want to mix up

00:33:27.644 --> 00:33:30.610
in the same locality different people

00:33:30.610 --> 00:33:33.869
with different needs,
and it's all the case.

00:33:33.869 --> 00:33:35.366
Evan's got kids, I've got kids,

00:33:35.366 --> 00:33:37.169
what I wanted to do when I was 21

00:33:37.169 --> 00:33:40.225
was very different when
I was 35 with kids.

00:33:40.225 --> 00:33:42.908
So, I think the other thing,
if you've got a big resort,

00:33:42.908 --> 00:33:44.354
then you could have the latitude

00:33:44.354 --> 00:33:48.070
to have different spaces
for different audiences.

00:33:48.070 --> 00:33:51.506
Again, it comes back to
really what is the audience

00:33:51.506 --> 00:33:54.035
you're serving, what is the segment?

00:33:54.035 --> 00:33:57.037
And then how do you
leverage your property,

00:33:57.037 --> 00:33:58.836
your resort, to do that best?

00:33:58.836 --> 00:34:01.278
And then also look at
the times of the year.

00:34:01.278 --> 00:34:02.831
You'll get very different people coming

00:34:02.831 --> 00:34:06.726
to the Caribbean during
hurricane season than you will do

00:34:06.726 --> 00:34:11.071
in that kind of January,
February, March period.

00:34:11.071 --> 00:34:12.313
So, I think it could be different.

00:34:12.313 --> 00:34:14.921
And the other thing, actually
Evan, this is different.

00:34:14.921 --> 00:34:17.312
When you have loads of resorts, do this.

00:34:17.312 --> 00:34:19.564
When you have off-peak times,
and I think you're right,

00:34:19.564 --> 00:34:23.268
you need the utilization,
you need the occupancy,

00:34:23.268 --> 00:34:26.821
then you can probably target
specialist interest groups.

00:34:26.821 --> 00:34:30.883
So, you could do yoga weeks,
or you could do Pilates,

00:34:30.883 --> 00:34:32.887
and you could have a
completely different audience

00:34:32.887 --> 00:34:35.903
and make sure that the
resorts kit it out for that.

00:34:35.903 --> 00:34:38.631
But that's a great way in low occupancy,

00:34:38.631 --> 00:34:42.049
and in bad off-season
times, to keep the occupancy

00:34:42.049 --> 00:34:43.833
up with different communities.

00:34:43.833 --> 00:34:45.382
But it all comes back to who you serve,

00:34:45.382 --> 00:34:49.346
and what's the audience
need, what are their wants,

00:34:49.346 --> 00:34:51.571
and how do you excel at them?

00:34:51.571 --> 00:34:52.663
- Yeah, and I think
sometimes you just have

00:34:52.663 --> 00:34:54.566
to turn away the wrong customer.

00:34:54.566 --> 00:34:56.790
If it's not a fit for you.

00:34:56.790 --> 00:34:59.619
If all Sage had was Sage One,

00:34:59.619 --> 00:35:02.226
which is the kind of entry level package,

00:35:02.226 --> 00:35:03.688
you're not going to go to IBM and say,

00:35:03.688 --> 00:35:06.224
hey, buy our Sage One
software, it's just not a fit.

00:35:06.224 --> 00:35:08.624
It's not going to be a great
experience for anybody.

00:35:08.624 --> 00:35:11.802
And so yes, it might
bring short-term revenue

00:35:11.802 --> 00:35:15.157
into your business, but
there's going to be complaints,

00:35:15.157 --> 00:35:17.067
you're going to get
negative scores everywhere,

00:35:17.067 --> 00:35:18.831
it's going to be a hassle for your staff,

00:35:18.831 --> 00:35:21.149
all your customers are going
to be unsatisfied with it.

00:35:21.149 --> 00:35:24.951
And so if you understand who
your ideal target market is,

00:35:24.951 --> 00:35:27.056
then just go strong after them

00:35:27.056 --> 00:35:29.298
and give them the best
possible experience.

00:35:29.298 --> 00:35:32.526
That means, to Stephen's
point about prioritizing,

00:35:32.526 --> 00:35:36.612
and the Greek word, and
coming down the one thing,

00:35:36.612 --> 00:35:39.382
it means that some customers
are not going to be perfect fits

00:35:39.382 --> 00:35:41.313
for your business, and that's okay.

00:35:41.313 --> 00:35:42.922
'Cause if you try to appeal to everybody,

00:35:42.922 --> 00:35:47.282
it means you're going to
strongly appeal to nobody.

00:35:47.282 --> 00:35:48.901
Let's hit up Paula.

00:35:48.901 --> 00:35:52.324
Paula: "I have a question!"
Exclamation point.

00:35:52.324 --> 00:35:55.281
"When your client
portfolio grows to a point

00:35:55.281 --> 00:35:57.737
"where you realize that you need help,

00:35:57.737 --> 00:35:59.383
"what tasks do you believe are critical

00:35:59.383 --> 00:36:01.822
"for you to continue handling yourselves?"

00:36:01.822 --> 00:36:04.380
So, she's struggling to kind of grow.

00:36:04.380 --> 00:36:07.224
It's a good place to be,
congratulations, Paula.

00:36:07.224 --> 00:36:08.310
She's grown her client portfolio

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:09.981
to a certain size, and she needs help.

00:36:09.981 --> 00:36:11.077
What should she be looking for,

00:36:11.077 --> 00:36:14.069
and how should she be spending her time?

00:36:14.069 --> 00:36:15.654
- Yeah, I think it's a
great question, Paula.

00:36:15.654 --> 00:36:17.462
And obviously, Paula, congratulations

00:36:17.462 --> 00:36:19.566
on the growth of your business.

00:36:19.566 --> 00:36:20.912
That's fantastic.

00:36:20.912 --> 00:36:22.864
I'd say things that really matter

00:36:22.864 --> 00:36:26.286
is things that touch the customer.

00:36:26.286 --> 00:36:28.957
Typically you'll find, Paula, you'll know

00:36:28.957 --> 00:36:30.949
are you a really customer oriented,

00:36:30.949 --> 00:36:33.815
customer experience, sales
marketing type person?

00:36:33.815 --> 00:36:37.335
Or are you like a product genius?

00:36:37.335 --> 00:36:39.459
Like a Steve Jobs.

00:36:39.459 --> 00:36:42.126
Where you really have the ideas.

00:36:43.041 --> 00:36:46.638
You rarely find the same person
with both sets of skills,

00:36:46.638 --> 00:36:49.797
so you may surround
yourself, you typically have

00:36:49.797 --> 00:36:51.267
partnerships started
where you've got someone

00:36:51.267 --> 00:36:54.280
who's very externally
focused and someone who does

00:36:54.280 --> 00:36:57.466
either all the back
office, all the inventing.

00:36:57.466 --> 00:37:00.149
What I would say is the first
areas you should look to

00:37:00.149 --> 00:37:04.316
bring in other folks, which
should be very easy towards me,

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:06.970
is things like what we call

00:37:06.970 --> 00:37:10.882
the back office finance,
paying your taxes.

00:37:10.882 --> 00:37:13.826
And obviously we've got a
great network of people like

00:37:13.826 --> 00:37:17.319
accountants and bookkeepers
who can help you with that.

00:37:17.319 --> 00:37:20.350
And you typically pay them
a retain or an annual fee

00:37:20.350 --> 00:37:24.304
and they do all the billing,
invoicing, cash collection,

00:37:24.304 --> 00:37:25.927
all those sort of things, to make sure

00:37:25.927 --> 00:37:28.338
you've got great cash
flow in your business.

00:37:28.338 --> 00:37:31.737
The other things that then
you can start thinking about

00:37:31.737 --> 00:37:33.916
is on some of the really specialist areas,

00:37:33.916 --> 00:37:38.408
which are really cool and do
give you big differentiation.

00:37:38.408 --> 00:37:41.799
And I'll pick out one area
like that is digital marketing.

00:37:41.799 --> 00:37:45.189
There are genius rock stars, and there are

00:37:45.189 --> 00:37:48.141
some agencies who have
got genius rock stars,

00:37:48.141 --> 00:37:52.141
who can do amazing stuff
around SC optimization,

00:37:53.336 --> 00:37:55.452
search engine optimization,
all these sort of things,

00:37:55.452 --> 00:37:58.662
social listening, social media.

00:37:58.662 --> 00:38:01.271
But that's a great area
where you can be involved,

00:38:01.271 --> 00:38:03.833
over see it, set the direction,

00:38:03.833 --> 00:38:05.869
set the mile stones and all that stuff.

00:38:05.869 --> 00:38:07.606
But bring in external people.

00:38:07.606 --> 00:38:10.949
But I would think the thing you
have to keep hold of, Paula,

00:38:10.949 --> 00:38:12.808
is the essence of the customer,

00:38:12.808 --> 00:38:15.970
and keeping that customer absolutely.

00:38:15.970 --> 00:38:17.940
I wouldn't advocate that you outsource

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:20.326
or bring in third parties to do that.

00:38:20.326 --> 00:38:22.380
'Cause that's going to be always critical

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:25.312
to you having that customer experience

00:38:25.312 --> 00:38:28.145
and the advocacy that you can get.

00:38:29.227 --> 00:38:32.049
- I think if you're looking
at building on a team,

00:38:32.049 --> 00:38:34.452
that's an exciting part
as an entrepreneur.

00:38:34.452 --> 00:38:35.758
The first thing I'd actually do is

00:38:35.758 --> 00:38:38.292
before looking at hiring
somebody, I would look at,

00:38:38.292 --> 00:38:39.845
what are the tasks that I'm great at,

00:38:39.845 --> 00:38:41.539
and where do I want to
spend most of my time

00:38:41.539 --> 00:38:44.289
that I am genius-level talent at?

00:38:45.174 --> 00:38:47.326
And that's where I need to
spend as much time as I can,

00:38:47.326 --> 00:38:49.671
and then bring people on to support it.

00:38:49.671 --> 00:38:52.046
But even before hiring I would look at,

00:38:52.046 --> 00:38:54.148
can I automate some of
the things in my business?

00:38:54.148 --> 00:38:55.161
Like, it's insane how many people

00:38:55.161 --> 00:38:58.334
still use Excel to do their accounting.

00:38:58.334 --> 00:39:00.861
Just get accounting software,
it's going to save so much time.

00:39:00.861 --> 00:39:04.025
And that sounds self-serving,
doing what we're doing here,

00:39:04.025 --> 00:39:05.270
but it's 100% true.

00:39:05.270 --> 00:39:06.974
Like, a lot of the tasks that you're doing

00:39:06.974 --> 00:39:09.389
should just be automated
and not given to somebody.

00:39:09.389 --> 00:39:11.467
When you are thinking
of bringing somebody on,

00:39:11.467 --> 00:39:13.017
you've identified the tasks that need

00:39:13.017 --> 00:39:14.985
to get done in the business,
that can't be automated,

00:39:14.985 --> 00:39:17.527
that has to go to a person,
but it shouldn't be you

00:39:17.527 --> 00:39:18.570
'cause you want to spend your time

00:39:18.570 --> 00:39:20.248
doing these higher level things.

00:39:20.248 --> 00:39:22.516
Those tasks are what you want to delegate.

00:39:22.516 --> 00:39:23.882
And then when you bring them on,

00:39:23.882 --> 00:39:26.482
you have to understand
that now you're a leader.

00:39:26.482 --> 00:39:28.723
You're managing a human being.

00:39:28.723 --> 00:39:31.323
It's not just, this happens
to so many entrepreneurs,

00:39:31.323 --> 00:39:32.531
they hire the wrong person 'cause it's not

00:39:32.531 --> 00:39:34.248
a good values fit as well.

00:39:34.248 --> 00:39:35.846
You think, okay I bring this person on,

00:39:35.846 --> 00:39:37.559
they're going to save
all this time for me,

00:39:37.559 --> 00:39:41.442
I'm going to off load all my work to them.

00:39:41.442 --> 00:39:43.027
And then you don't guide them,

00:39:43.027 --> 00:39:44.418
and you don't make time for them.

00:39:44.418 --> 00:39:45.946
The first month of working
with them is going to take

00:39:45.946 --> 00:39:49.412
a lot of your time to train
them, and manage them,

00:39:49.412 --> 00:39:51.477
and understand them, and guide them.

00:39:51.477 --> 00:39:55.223
And so recognizing that your
role is now going to be a leader,

00:39:55.223 --> 00:39:57.391
and helping that person
reach their potential

00:39:57.391 --> 00:40:00.196
is much more than just them
accomplishing them tasks

00:40:00.196 --> 00:40:05.027
that will save you time,
is super, super important.

00:40:05.027 --> 00:40:05.933
How much time do you think--

00:40:05.933 --> 00:40:06.766
- Another thing is, Paula--

00:40:06.766 --> 00:40:07.599
- Yeah.

00:40:07.599 --> 00:40:08.432
No, no go for it.

00:40:08.432 --> 00:40:09.490
- Just, sorry, sorry Evan.

00:40:09.490 --> 00:40:11.181
Just two other things, really.

00:40:11.181 --> 00:40:13.437
Is I'd always advocate,
especially when you start

00:40:13.437 --> 00:40:15.442
early stage of the company,

00:40:15.442 --> 00:40:19.296
that you bring in, if you're
some marketing individual

00:40:19.296 --> 00:40:21.855
or someone to help with the automation,

00:40:21.855 --> 00:40:24.631
cash flow box, and all that sort of stuff.

00:40:24.631 --> 00:40:28.567
Try and maybe it's one
day every two weeks.

00:40:28.567 --> 00:40:30.859
So you don't kind of jump
into it with both feet.

00:40:30.859 --> 00:40:32.682
And the other thing I'd always say

00:40:32.682 --> 00:40:33.863
is it's a really cool thing,

00:40:33.863 --> 00:40:35.349
if you're going to look
at someone on sales

00:40:35.349 --> 00:40:37.230
and marketing for your business,

00:40:37.230 --> 00:40:40.057
it'd be a really cool thing
to, we call them interim,

00:40:40.057 --> 00:40:43.494
so bring someone in on an interim
contract for three months,

00:40:43.494 --> 00:40:45.548
and if they work out,
and it'd be really good,

00:40:45.548 --> 00:40:47.724
then make them permanent.

00:40:47.724 --> 00:40:49.981
So, you can have these sort
of interim to permanent roles.

00:40:49.981 --> 00:40:53.253
And just the concept there
is try before you buy.

00:40:53.253 --> 00:40:55.838
So, Evan's right, when
you bring someone new

00:40:55.838 --> 00:40:57.992
you want them to express the essence

00:40:57.992 --> 00:41:00.529
as if they were you, Paula.

00:41:00.529 --> 00:41:02.893
And that's very powerful.

00:41:02.893 --> 00:41:05.935
And live your values,
all those sort of things.

00:41:05.935 --> 00:41:07.712
And a great way, the
only way to find that,

00:41:07.712 --> 00:41:09.302
and you can't do that
through interviews and stuff

00:41:09.302 --> 00:41:12.030
or references really, it's hard.

00:41:12.030 --> 00:41:14.103
And recruitment, you know
this, when you're kind of

00:41:14.103 --> 00:41:16.681
starting a company, you
hire your first five people,

00:41:16.681 --> 00:41:18.826
you don't probably get
it right all the time.

00:41:18.826 --> 00:41:21.098
And it really slows you
down, you can cost yourself

00:41:21.098 --> 00:41:23.214
twelve months on these sort of things.

00:41:23.214 --> 00:41:27.610
So, a pretty cool way of doing
it is try before you buy.

00:41:27.610 --> 00:41:30.693
Or for even a two week trial period.

00:41:30.693 --> 00:41:32.798
And then you do find
out when you give them

00:41:32.798 --> 00:41:35.617
some real work to do if
they share your values

00:41:35.617 --> 00:41:37.769
and share your dreams and can deliver

00:41:37.769 --> 00:41:41.375
the acceleration you want
and expand your business.

00:41:41.375 --> 00:41:42.208
- I love it.

00:41:42.208 --> 00:41:45.485
What is the onboarding
process look like at Sage?

00:41:45.485 --> 00:41:46.685
Like somebody's been hired, you go through

00:41:46.685 --> 00:41:47.794
the job interview process.

00:41:47.794 --> 00:41:50.627
Okay, day one in terms of training

00:41:51.486 --> 00:41:54.016
and introducing to the
team and getting involved,

00:41:54.016 --> 00:41:57.266
what does onboarding look like for you?

00:41:58.338 --> 00:41:59.716
- Everybody gets the one day.

00:41:59.716 --> 00:42:01.718
So all colleagues come in and get one day,

00:42:01.718 --> 00:42:05.040
and they either get flown
up to Atlanta or Big Hubs

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:09.169
in the UK, where we
get everybody together,

00:42:09.169 --> 00:42:13.128
typically on the first
Monday of every month.

00:42:13.128 --> 00:42:14.411
And that's the one day, and that's about

00:42:14.411 --> 00:42:16.297
the history of the
company, what we care about

00:42:16.297 --> 00:42:19.113
in terms of our customers,
a bit about the products,

00:42:19.113 --> 00:42:20.874
and then a lot of culture values,

00:42:20.874 --> 00:42:25.274
and where to go sort of
thing, so they find it.

00:42:25.274 --> 00:42:26.639
When they leave that, they should be able

00:42:26.639 --> 00:42:29.188
to navigate 'round the company
and know where people are

00:42:29.188 --> 00:42:31.725
and what we really care
about around customers.

00:42:31.725 --> 00:42:34.701
Then for managers they get three days.

00:42:34.701 --> 00:42:35.971
And they get things like
digital development,

00:42:35.971 --> 00:42:38.037
and all that sort of
stuff that's part of that.

00:42:38.037 --> 00:42:40.105
And then for sales people
and product people,

00:42:40.105 --> 00:42:42.141
they typically get five days.

00:42:42.141 --> 00:42:44.917
So, I'll be honest Evan, it's an area

00:42:44.917 --> 00:42:48.283
where I think we could do a lot better.

00:42:48.283 --> 00:42:50.646
I think if we have this
conversation in three years time,

00:42:50.646 --> 00:42:52.662
you'll probably find in Sage,

00:42:52.662 --> 00:42:54.543
we'd want to invest probably three days

00:42:54.543 --> 00:42:56.951
for all our colleagues
joining at that stage.

00:42:56.951 --> 00:42:59.022
'Cause I think, Paula, the other thing is,

00:42:59.022 --> 00:43:03.070
it's so easy to hire someone,
you get 'em a coffee,

00:43:03.070 --> 00:43:06.774
you say, there you go, here's
a laptop, and off you go.

00:43:06.774 --> 00:43:10.818
I would absolutely beg you
to invest at least a minimum

00:43:10.818 --> 00:43:13.951
in the morning, and tell 'em
about what your beliefs are,

00:43:13.951 --> 00:43:16.413
your ideas, your values, what do you want

00:43:16.413 --> 00:43:18.683
to do with customers, you know.

00:43:18.683 --> 00:43:21.231
And then break all that down and say,

00:43:21.231 --> 00:43:24.833
and the next 30 days I think
we have to do these things,

00:43:24.833 --> 00:43:26.215
and the next 90 days we have to do,

00:43:26.215 --> 00:43:27.946
so there are very much results

00:43:27.946 --> 00:43:30.384
around what matters for customers.

00:43:30.384 --> 00:43:32.699
But it is work just taking that time out,

00:43:32.699 --> 00:43:35.091
even though it's crazy, and it's busy,

00:43:35.091 --> 00:43:37.658
just to really onboard 'em,

00:43:37.658 --> 00:43:40.676
and get them aligned with
your way of thinking.

00:43:40.676 --> 00:43:41.948
- I love it.

00:43:41.948 --> 00:43:42.781
Alright, next.

00:43:42.781 --> 00:43:45.760
Peter: "Hey Evan and
Stephen, what technology

00:43:45.760 --> 00:43:48.444
"or programs would you
recommend to stand out

00:43:48.444 --> 00:43:51.444
"from the rest in customer service?"

00:43:52.629 --> 00:43:54.522
- Oh wow, Peter, this is,

00:43:54.522 --> 00:43:56.224
so I think around customer service there's

00:43:56.224 --> 00:43:58.212
some companies who do this
typically pretty badly,

00:43:58.212 --> 00:44:02.490
so the people who have done
this badly maybe invested

00:44:02.490 --> 00:44:05.670
billions in, is typically
banking and financial services.

00:44:05.670 --> 00:44:09.332
So U.S. data, and now I think
the same with European data,

00:44:09.332 --> 00:44:12.082
only 38% of banks' customers

00:44:12.990 --> 00:44:16.249
feel that they're valued or
rewarded for their custom.

00:44:16.249 --> 00:44:18.194
So, that's the bad situation.

00:44:18.194 --> 00:44:21.381
And to Evan's point
around resourcefulness,

00:44:21.381 --> 00:44:22.964
and they've spent billions.

00:44:22.964 --> 00:44:25.388
Now, what I would say on the positive

00:44:25.388 --> 00:44:27.548
is there's some really cool technology.

00:44:27.548 --> 00:44:29.806
I've talked about social listening,

00:44:29.806 --> 00:44:33.730
there's some really
amazing start up companies,

00:44:33.730 --> 00:44:36.564
actually a few that we've
used internally around

00:44:36.564 --> 00:44:40.282
taking the pulse of
customers, and you can do,

00:44:40.282 --> 00:44:42.680
what I'd advocate the future is all about,

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:46.234
taking the pulse at every interaction.

00:44:46.234 --> 00:44:48.759
And customers are more
and more used to this.

00:44:48.759 --> 00:44:52.117
And when you kind of build your
system of customer journey,

00:44:52.117 --> 00:44:53.937
it's really important at every stage

00:44:53.937 --> 00:44:56.444
that you try and exceed
their expectations.

00:44:56.444 --> 00:45:00.611
So, it's kind of not a one
hit, one size fits all.

00:45:01.454 --> 00:45:03.450
It is a very much a journey,

00:45:03.450 --> 00:45:05.512
and I think there's technology I've,

00:45:05.512 --> 00:45:08.600
actually a company we've
been using, Rant and Rave,

00:45:08.600 --> 00:45:11.595
good name there, Rant and Rave,

00:45:11.595 --> 00:45:13.534
is a company where they do pulsing

00:45:13.534 --> 00:45:16.443
of customers and real time feedback,

00:45:16.443 --> 00:45:18.943
and also all the storing of...

00:45:20.027 --> 00:45:22.527
We use methodology internally,

00:45:23.711 --> 00:45:27.336
it's a big step forward,
but it's not the end game,

00:45:27.336 --> 00:45:31.568
it's called Net Promoter
Scores, and people call it NPS,

00:45:31.568 --> 00:45:33.895
and it's really simple, it's
basically would you recommend

00:45:33.895 --> 00:45:37.812
this to your friends and
other people in your..

00:45:41.059 --> 00:45:44.593
And they're either advocates,
and they'd recommend it,

00:45:44.593 --> 00:45:46.951
or they're neutral, or they're detractors.

00:45:46.951 --> 00:45:49.081
So, it's quite a pretty simple measure.

00:45:49.081 --> 00:45:52.547
And I think the simplicity and the beauty

00:45:52.547 --> 00:45:55.966
of these sort of tools are
when they actually grab

00:45:55.966 --> 00:45:59.983
the sentiment at every
stage of the journey,

00:45:59.983 --> 00:46:02.171
and when they make it really
simple that you can have

00:46:02.171 --> 00:46:06.461
actionable content that
you can deliver on.

00:46:06.461 --> 00:46:09.753
Therefore obviously,
Peter, the value you get

00:46:09.753 --> 00:46:11.782
is when you can change your process

00:46:11.782 --> 00:46:15.633
or how you handle a customer's situation,

00:46:15.633 --> 00:46:19.511
how they take your products,
it could be anything.

00:46:19.511 --> 00:46:20.826
It could be the packaging on the products,

00:46:20.826 --> 00:46:23.080
it could be any aspect of the experience

00:46:23.080 --> 00:46:24.134
that matters to them.

00:46:24.134 --> 00:46:26.355
But it's really useful
for us as entrepreneurs

00:46:26.355 --> 00:46:29.553
to know how do we change
it, how do we improve it.

00:46:29.553 --> 00:46:33.408
And then also test drive
it with a new prototype,

00:46:33.408 --> 00:46:35.991
and then also get new feedback.

00:46:37.140 --> 00:46:37.973
- I like it.

00:46:37.973 --> 00:46:39.346
I think to add to it I would say,

00:46:39.346 --> 00:46:42.694
a lot of the, from a
technology perspective,

00:46:42.694 --> 00:46:45.445
a lot of the interactions
are even behind the scenes.

00:46:45.445 --> 00:46:47.554
You know, things like
making your website faster

00:46:47.554 --> 00:46:49.279
so I have a better experience visiting it.

00:46:49.279 --> 00:46:51.815
Things like does it take 25 steps to

00:46:51.815 --> 00:46:54.368
buy your product or get in touch with you.

00:46:54.368 --> 00:46:57.777
It's a lot of the stuff that
people want it automated,

00:46:57.777 --> 00:46:59.273
you want to save them
time, so look through

00:46:59.273 --> 00:47:03.112
all your processes and think,
can I make this better?

00:47:03.112 --> 00:47:05.788
I would also say, at the core of it,

00:47:05.788 --> 00:47:07.621
at some point there's going to be

00:47:07.621 --> 00:47:09.759
an interaction with a human being.

00:47:09.759 --> 00:47:11.778
You can only automate so much.

00:47:11.778 --> 00:47:13.390
When somebody has a
problem or somebody wants

00:47:13.390 --> 00:47:15.587
to say thank you or
somebody has a question,

00:47:15.587 --> 00:47:17.984
they want to be able
to reach a human being.

00:47:17.984 --> 00:47:19.654
And personally for me it drives me nuts

00:47:19.654 --> 00:47:23.104
when I have to call in to a help line,

00:47:23.104 --> 00:47:25.587
and then it's like, press
one of these five options,

00:47:25.587 --> 00:47:26.789
and then one of these two options,

00:47:26.789 --> 00:47:30.063
it's like you're cycled
through this 20 step process

00:47:30.063 --> 00:47:31.963
before you actually get
to talk to a human being.

00:47:31.963 --> 00:47:34.400
And so I would just look at what can I do

00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:37.640
to make the human interaction better.

00:47:37.640 --> 00:47:40.148
And sometimes the answer's
just get more people,

00:47:40.148 --> 00:47:42.168
as opposed to scale the technology.

00:47:42.168 --> 00:47:44.021
Like I look at my YouTube
channel as an example,

00:47:44.021 --> 00:47:46.424
we get hundreds of comments everyday,

00:47:46.424 --> 00:47:48.554
and I want everybody to get a response.

00:47:48.554 --> 00:47:50.795
And I don't want an automated response

00:47:50.795 --> 00:47:51.747
that goes to everyone that says

00:47:51.747 --> 00:47:53.418
thank you for commenting,
thank you for commenting.

00:47:53.418 --> 00:47:54.892
I want a human being to respond.

00:47:54.892 --> 00:47:56.710
And so, I've hired two people on my team,

00:47:56.710 --> 00:47:58.101
and that's their job is

00:47:58.101 --> 00:47:59.351
they go through the comments everyday,

00:47:59.351 --> 00:48:00.745
and they respond to everybody,

00:48:00.745 --> 00:48:03.328
and they save the ones that
I need to see for me as well,

00:48:03.328 --> 00:48:05.162
'cause I don't have
the time to go through,

00:48:05.162 --> 00:48:08.226
it's like two people's full time job.

00:48:08.226 --> 00:48:11.635
And so sometimes the answer's
just scaling people as well.

00:48:11.635 --> 00:48:13.223
And the technology that
you bring on should be

00:48:13.223 --> 00:48:15.716
to make the jobs of your people easier

00:48:15.716 --> 00:48:18.012
to be able to have those interactions.

00:48:18.012 --> 00:48:19.339
- So Evan, a question for you is,

00:48:19.339 --> 00:48:21.011
with those two guys you brought on,

00:48:21.011 --> 00:48:24.656
is how do you get them
to answer the questions

00:48:24.656 --> 00:48:26.938
as if they were Evan?

00:48:26.938 --> 00:48:27.771
- Uhh.

00:48:27.771 --> 00:48:28.604
- What do you invest in them?

00:48:28.604 --> 00:48:31.818
When they joined, how did
you sit them down and say,

00:48:31.818 --> 00:48:32.958
this is my philosophy?

00:48:32.958 --> 00:48:34.879
What did you do about that?

00:48:34.879 --> 00:48:37.140
- So, my hiring process already eliminates

00:48:37.140 --> 00:48:39.717
most of the people who wouldn't be a fit.

00:48:39.717 --> 00:48:42.105
It'll start off with, "I'm
looking for customer service rep

00:48:42.105 --> 00:48:43.482
"who believes in entrepreneurs,"

00:48:43.482 --> 00:48:46.080
and the first two paragraphs
would all be about the mission.

00:48:46.080 --> 00:48:47.345
And some people will look at that and say,

00:48:47.345 --> 00:48:49.554
that's the stupidest thing ever.

00:48:49.554 --> 00:48:50.506
It's like, great!

00:48:50.506 --> 00:48:51.898
We're not going to get along.

00:48:51.898 --> 00:48:53.904
This is not going to be a good fit.

00:48:53.904 --> 00:48:55.675
Please don't apply, because we're going to

00:48:55.675 --> 00:48:57.480
both be wasting our time.

00:48:57.480 --> 00:48:59.843
And so a lot of the non-believers

00:48:59.843 --> 00:49:01.544
are already kind of filtered out.

00:49:01.544 --> 00:49:03.773
From there I just look
at, obviously people

00:49:03.773 --> 00:49:05.362
that understand the context, you know,

00:49:05.362 --> 00:49:09.295
English language writing
properly, all that kind of stuff.

00:49:09.295 --> 00:49:11.919
But I don't have them respond as me.

00:49:11.919 --> 00:49:13.630
They always put their name at the end.

00:49:13.630 --> 00:49:14.463
I don't want people thinking

00:49:14.463 --> 00:49:17.574
that I'm responding when it's not me.

00:49:17.574 --> 00:49:19.303
And so they always put
their name at the end.

00:49:19.303 --> 00:49:22.073
But they believe in belief.

00:49:22.073 --> 00:49:23.484
They believe in the mission.

00:49:23.484 --> 00:49:26.226
They don't always say it the
same way that I would say it,

00:49:26.226 --> 00:49:28.095
often times they're a
lot nicer than I would be

00:49:28.095 --> 00:49:31.228
to somebody who's leaving a comment.

00:49:31.228 --> 00:49:32.946
And so I want them to be themselves

00:49:32.946 --> 00:49:34.658
but still belief in a mission.

00:49:34.658 --> 00:49:37.145
I think if you turn people into robots,

00:49:37.145 --> 00:49:39.250
then they're going to act like robots.

00:49:39.250 --> 00:49:41.950
Where I want people to
believe in what I believe,

00:49:41.950 --> 00:49:44.017
and then use their
judgment and their heart

00:49:44.017 --> 00:49:45.911
to solve it as they would solve it.

00:49:45.911 --> 00:49:47.632
And it's always worked out.

00:49:47.632 --> 00:49:49.498
And then they save the
ones that I need to see,

00:49:49.498 --> 00:49:51.080
suggestions for the channel,

00:49:51.080 --> 00:49:53.567
complaints about something that I did,

00:49:53.567 --> 00:49:54.853
that's good feedback for me to be able

00:49:54.853 --> 00:49:57.447
to apply and learn from.

00:49:57.447 --> 00:49:58.748
- That's really good,
and I think that's really

00:49:58.748 --> 00:50:00.243
authentic as well, Evan.

00:50:00.243 --> 00:50:03.826
The other thing for Peter
is, if it's like a product

00:50:03.826 --> 00:50:06.749
that you have, then
I'd absolutely advocate

00:50:06.749 --> 00:50:08.805
within your early customers you create

00:50:08.805 --> 00:50:11.629
some sort of earlier adopter program.

00:50:11.629 --> 00:50:15.144
And this is a program where you
directly get your customers,

00:50:15.144 --> 00:50:16.980
like Evan said on the website,

00:50:16.980 --> 00:50:20.325
it's so easy when we have an idea.

00:50:20.325 --> 00:50:23.157
We might not be amazing website designers,

00:50:23.157 --> 00:50:24.522
but you've got to put yourselves

00:50:24.522 --> 00:50:25.645
in the shoes of the customers.

00:50:25.645 --> 00:50:27.555
So, what better way to do that

00:50:27.555 --> 00:50:29.068
than have an early adopter program

00:50:29.068 --> 00:50:31.696
where you get your customers
to give you real time feedback,

00:50:31.696 --> 00:50:34.808
maybe with Skype, on what experience

00:50:34.808 --> 00:50:35.882
they're getting on the website,

00:50:35.882 --> 00:50:37.414
and you just take 'em through it.

00:50:37.414 --> 00:50:39.592
And there's loads of ways
you can reward 'em for that.

00:50:39.592 --> 00:50:41.781
And it makes your customers
feel really special.

00:50:41.781 --> 00:50:46.451
So, I would constantly
innovate your product through,

00:50:46.451 --> 00:50:48.628
and your customer service obviously,

00:50:48.628 --> 00:50:53.015
through getting direct
customer sentiment and feeling,

00:50:53.015 --> 00:50:56.018
real time through some sort of structured,

00:50:56.018 --> 00:50:58.426
early adopter program that
the customers typically,

00:50:58.426 --> 00:51:01.445
I've found, love to be involved in.

00:51:01.445 --> 00:51:02.484
- Love it.

00:51:02.484 --> 00:51:05.985
Next up is Joy: "Sometimes
it's hard to keep up with

00:51:05.985 --> 00:51:08.284
"all the different customer questions.

00:51:08.284 --> 00:51:11.457
"How do you know how to prioritize?"

00:51:11.457 --> 00:51:13.797
- That's a great question, Joy.

00:51:13.797 --> 00:51:17.838
And I think you've got to,
there's probably two categories,

00:51:17.838 --> 00:51:21.647
there's stuff where it's
just a one-line answer.

00:51:21.647 --> 00:51:24.509
And actually what I find,

00:51:24.509 --> 00:51:27.399
particularly when things get crazy,

00:51:27.399 --> 00:51:29.899
and the volumes just go spike,

00:51:31.463 --> 00:51:35.451
some entrepreneurs, and this
is quite naturally human,

00:51:35.451 --> 00:51:37.419
can just freeze and they think, oh my,

00:51:37.419 --> 00:51:40.086
OMG, how do I handle this stuff?

00:51:41.040 --> 00:51:44.207
And typically, we use JDI, just do it.

00:51:45.067 --> 00:51:49.157
So there's some stuff, you
probably 50 or even 80%,

00:51:49.157 --> 00:51:52.017
you can just carve them out and just say

00:51:52.017 --> 00:51:55.596
one-line response, boom,
and it's put to bed.

00:51:55.596 --> 00:51:57.887
And it takes a lot of the noise out of it.

00:51:57.887 --> 00:51:59.886
And then I think there's
kind of two categories,

00:51:59.886 --> 00:52:02.546
there's one category, and it's almost like

00:52:02.546 --> 00:52:04.170
a sort of two-dimensional chart,

00:52:04.170 --> 00:52:06.619
where you've got something
that's really important,

00:52:06.619 --> 00:52:09.075
and the other dimension is urgency.

00:52:09.075 --> 00:52:11.032
Timeliness and urgency.

00:52:11.032 --> 00:52:15.199
And I think as entrepreneurs
we often spend a lot of time

00:52:16.634 --> 00:52:19.710
on non-important stuff
that seems to be urgent.

00:52:19.710 --> 00:52:22.393
And what we should do is really prioritize

00:52:22.393 --> 00:52:24.471
the really important stuff.

00:52:24.471 --> 00:52:27.661
So, is it a new customer that's
going to drive more revenue,

00:52:27.661 --> 00:52:31.499
or be a big, maybe it's
a rock star customer,

00:52:31.499 --> 00:52:32.811
who's going to be a big reference,

00:52:32.811 --> 00:52:35.129
who's a poster child in their industry.

00:52:35.129 --> 00:52:38.420
Wow, if that's the case,
that's really important.

00:52:38.420 --> 00:52:39.592
And then you need to determine,

00:52:39.592 --> 00:52:41.416
once you've determined
the level of importance,

00:52:41.416 --> 00:52:44.578
and that's a nano-second
decision that takes two seconds,

00:52:44.578 --> 00:52:46.778
is literally then, on
the level of urgency,

00:52:46.778 --> 00:52:48.932
and then prioritize it according into

00:52:48.932 --> 00:52:51.897
the most important, most urgent.

00:52:51.897 --> 00:52:54.175
And there could be important stuff,

00:52:54.175 --> 00:52:56.021
but it might not be
time critical for today,

00:52:56.021 --> 00:52:57.954
and you can do that in 30, 60, 90 days.

00:52:57.954 --> 00:53:02.378
So, I'd just apply that
sort of lens to the inbound

00:53:02.378 --> 00:53:05.292
to give you the clarity,
the prioritization.

00:53:05.292 --> 00:53:06.125
- I like it.

00:53:06.125 --> 00:53:08.424
I would add, if you're getting overwhelmed

00:53:08.424 --> 00:53:10.893
by how many emails are coming in,

00:53:10.893 --> 00:53:14.075
I would look at it, is my process okay?

00:53:14.075 --> 00:53:17.058
Can I just make my website more clear?

00:53:17.058 --> 00:53:18.784
So that I don't get as many,

00:53:18.784 --> 00:53:21.622
'cause people don't want to
write in with a question.

00:53:21.622 --> 00:53:24.264
They don't want to write
in asking for help.

00:53:24.264 --> 00:53:26.323
So, can you make your process better?

00:53:26.323 --> 00:53:28.524
Because for every one
person who does write in,

00:53:28.524 --> 00:53:31.364
there's another ten who just don't bother,

00:53:31.364 --> 00:53:33.291
they don't want to spend the time.

00:53:33.291 --> 00:53:35.066
And so if you make your process better,

00:53:35.066 --> 00:53:37.325
then you won't have to
deal with as much inbound.

00:53:37.325 --> 00:53:40.481
I think to then handle the rest of them,

00:53:40.481 --> 00:53:43.032
you have to identify what your
priorities are as a company,

00:53:43.032 --> 00:53:45.427
here's where we want to grow,
and here's how it fits in,

00:53:45.427 --> 00:53:47.159
and if you're getting asked the same thing

00:53:47.159 --> 00:53:49.144
over and over and over and over again,

00:53:49.144 --> 00:53:50.333
hey, maybe that's the indication that

00:53:50.333 --> 00:53:52.651
this is a new thing we should add.

00:53:52.651 --> 00:53:54.688
I'm sure at Sage people write
in all the time and say,

00:53:54.688 --> 00:53:56.789
hey, it'd be great to add this
feature, and this feature,

00:53:56.789 --> 00:53:58.781
and we'd love to see
this, and can you include.

00:53:58.781 --> 00:54:00.114
Everybody has their opinions on

00:54:00.114 --> 00:54:01.693
how you should run your company.

00:54:01.693 --> 00:54:04.304
You know, in their own
best interest, of course.

00:54:04.304 --> 00:54:06.343
And I think collecting
that feedback is important.

00:54:06.343 --> 00:54:09.403
Obviously, Sage is not going
to include every single feature

00:54:09.403 --> 00:54:12.271
that everybody has on their minds.

00:54:12.271 --> 00:54:14.756
But if you get the same thing
coming over and over again,

00:54:14.756 --> 00:54:17.302
that can help prioritize and say, hey,

00:54:17.302 --> 00:54:20.243
a good percentage of our customers
are asking this question,

00:54:20.243 --> 00:54:22.983
how do we pick the topics
for these sessions, right?

00:54:22.983 --> 00:54:26.065
Like a number of you have
similar kind of topics,

00:54:26.065 --> 00:54:28.749
suggestions in mind,
and so the one that is

00:54:28.749 --> 00:54:32.110
kind of most popular, that's
what we go with for the next one.

00:54:32.110 --> 00:54:35.613
I think too though, also the people who,

00:54:35.613 --> 00:54:37.296
just letting people know where they're at,

00:54:37.296 --> 00:54:38.591
letting people know what
the realistic chance

00:54:38.591 --> 00:54:40.025
of that happening are,

00:54:40.025 --> 00:54:43.566
and just having some empathy for them.

00:54:43.566 --> 00:54:48.117
For people who want my channels
translated into Spanish,

00:54:48.117 --> 00:54:50.050
you know, something we
get asked all the time.

00:54:50.050 --> 00:54:51.547
It's something that's on my radar,

00:54:51.547 --> 00:54:53.980
but it's not an immediate priority for me.

00:54:53.980 --> 00:54:55.504
And so I write back to them and say,

00:54:55.504 --> 00:54:56.916
thank you so much for the love,

00:54:56.916 --> 00:54:58.632
and I would love to be
able to create this content

00:54:58.632 --> 00:55:00.790
for you in Spanish so that you
could understand it better,

00:55:00.790 --> 00:55:04.039
and grow your business in
whatever country you're in.

00:55:04.039 --> 00:55:06.487
It's just not at the top
of my priority list yet,

00:55:06.487 --> 00:55:10.167
but the fact that you brought
it up, I'm going to remember it,

00:55:10.167 --> 00:55:11.706
and hopefully one day we'll get to it.

00:55:11.706 --> 00:55:13.534
Just recognizing them as opposed to

00:55:13.534 --> 00:55:15.071
them feeling like they're totally lost,

00:55:15.071 --> 00:55:17.376
even if they're never
going to get the thing

00:55:17.376 --> 00:55:21.029
that they're asking for,
I think is important.

00:55:21.029 --> 00:55:22.212
And I think that's the way to stand out,

00:55:22.212 --> 00:55:24.424
back to the kind of topic
we're covering today,

00:55:24.424 --> 00:55:25.957
standing out with your customers,

00:55:25.957 --> 00:55:28.945
I think it's really important.

00:55:28.945 --> 00:55:31.193
There must be tons of people
who write into Sage saying

00:55:31.193 --> 00:55:33.149
like, can you write this
feature and this feature.

00:55:33.149 --> 00:55:34.941
- Yeah, tens of thousands.

00:55:34.941 --> 00:55:38.385
And with that we have
sort of formal process,

00:55:38.385 --> 00:55:41.621
and it's kind of this,
we call it basically,

00:55:41.621 --> 00:55:44.176
the product marketeers
and management, anybody.

00:55:44.176 --> 00:55:45.009
And we love it.

00:55:45.009 --> 00:55:48.286
Well, actually I'd advocate
that I think, Evan and I,

00:55:48.286 --> 00:55:51.265
the more feedback we get
from customers, the better.

00:55:51.265 --> 00:55:53.721
Good, bad, indifferent, whatever it is,

00:55:53.721 --> 00:55:57.888
feedback is golden nuggets,
is absolutely magical.

00:56:01.140 --> 00:56:04.778
So, I'd absolutely advocate,
and I think with social media,

00:56:04.778 --> 00:56:08.382
social antenna, there's brilliant
ways to get feedback now.

00:56:08.382 --> 00:56:09.838
And then you got to act on it.

00:56:09.838 --> 00:56:11.735
And you're right, you're absolutely right,

00:56:11.735 --> 00:56:13.576
Joy, you've got to prioritize it.

00:56:13.576 --> 00:56:14.576
And I think to Evan and his point,

00:56:14.576 --> 00:56:16.526
people just value the honesty.

00:56:16.526 --> 00:56:19.277
If you say, that's important
but we can't do that

00:56:19.277 --> 00:56:22.236
for the next year or so,
they appreciate that.

00:56:22.236 --> 00:56:25.651
And then the important thing
is to put a process in to say,

00:56:25.651 --> 00:56:29.168
that'll remind on your
Google Calender or whatever,

00:56:29.168 --> 00:56:30.686
in a year's time, friend them back to say,

00:56:30.686 --> 00:56:31.622
we're ready to do it now.

00:56:31.622 --> 00:56:33.868
Or actually it's another six months away.

00:56:33.868 --> 00:56:37.107
People just like to be kept in touch.

00:56:37.107 --> 00:56:37.940
- I love it.

00:56:37.940 --> 00:56:39.678
So speaking of feedback, you're launching

00:56:39.678 --> 00:56:42.268
your vlog later on today.

00:56:42.268 --> 00:56:43.101
- [Stephen] Yeah.

00:56:43.101 --> 00:56:44.914
- We'll link it up,
'cause it's not live yet,

00:56:44.914 --> 00:56:48.073
but when it goes live, we'll
link up in the description.

00:56:48.073 --> 00:56:51.164
What kind of feedback
do you want from people

00:56:51.164 --> 00:56:55.331
on watching your first episode
of the Stephen Kelly Vlog?

00:56:56.966 --> 00:57:00.502
- Yeah, I think my purpose is two-fold.

00:57:00.502 --> 00:57:03.072
One is to make it useful for the audience.

00:57:03.072 --> 00:57:05.550
So, you can very quickly, guys out there,

00:57:05.550 --> 00:57:07.548
just tell me is this useful or not.

00:57:07.548 --> 00:57:10.706
And secondly, I want you to enjoy it.

00:57:10.706 --> 00:57:11.788
I want you to have fun.

00:57:11.788 --> 00:57:13.661
And watch it, and enjoy it, and think,

00:57:13.661 --> 00:57:15.404
oh, this is five minute of my time that's,

00:57:15.404 --> 00:57:17.604
yeah, it's good fun, I like it,

00:57:17.604 --> 00:57:20.307
and I got just one nugget
or one idea out of it.

00:57:20.307 --> 00:57:21.157
That's great.

00:57:21.157 --> 00:57:23.822
So, usefulness and enjoyment is important.

00:57:23.822 --> 00:57:26.256
And what would do us is
thinking about this, Evan,

00:57:26.256 --> 00:57:29.269
is when we're in Toronto in,

00:57:29.269 --> 00:57:31.864
actually at Sage Summit
at the back end of June,

00:57:31.864 --> 00:57:33.170
I don't know if they
guys are working on this,

00:57:33.170 --> 00:57:38.005
but we should do some fun
stuff, me and you for the vlog.

00:57:38.005 --> 00:57:41.373
We should do some, I'm maybe
going up the CNN tower,

00:57:41.373 --> 00:57:44.594
do some stuff there that
we could do that's fun.

00:57:44.594 --> 00:57:48.076
And also showcases your great
city as well, we love that.

00:57:48.076 --> 00:57:49.922
- I'm excited, I'm always down for that.

00:57:49.922 --> 00:57:51.705
So, we're at an hour.

00:57:51.705 --> 00:57:53.336
It's crazy how quickly it goes.

00:57:53.336 --> 00:57:55.709
Guys, and next time
we're coming to you live,

00:57:55.709 --> 00:57:59.121
it'll be together, me and
Stephen, at the end of June.

00:57:59.121 --> 00:58:00.452
So that'll be fun.

00:58:00.452 --> 00:58:02.537
Stephen, kind of final
words to wrap this up

00:58:02.537 --> 00:58:05.522
and put a bow on it for the audience?

00:58:05.522 --> 00:58:07.386
- Yeah, I just think something
that pulls all together,

00:58:07.386 --> 00:58:10.347
this is totally random
how this has happened,

00:58:10.347 --> 00:58:12.065
but one of our customers, Kitti Murray,

00:58:12.065 --> 00:58:15.907
who's the founder of
Refuge Coffee Company.

00:58:15.907 --> 00:58:20.161
She's given this quote where
it sort of says it all,

00:58:20.161 --> 00:58:23.547
"We believe in doing things that matter,

00:58:23.547 --> 00:58:27.205
"with people instead of for them."

00:58:27.205 --> 00:58:29.927
When you do things for people, you affirm

00:58:29.927 --> 00:58:31.907
their inherent dignity and value.

00:58:31.907 --> 00:58:34.229
You bring them into the
decision making table,

00:58:34.229 --> 00:58:36.449
and you make them feel very part of it.

00:58:36.449 --> 00:58:39.256
And that's why we do
things with our customers.

00:58:39.256 --> 00:58:40.256
Always with.

00:58:41.981 --> 00:58:42.814
- I like it.

00:58:42.814 --> 00:58:45.510
You might be starting the
trend of now having to end

00:58:45.510 --> 00:58:48.198
every show with a quote
as well as start, Stephen.

00:58:48.198 --> 00:58:49.301
People are going to like those.

00:58:49.301 --> 00:58:51.173
(laughing)

00:58:51.173 --> 00:58:52.061
Well, thank you guys.

00:58:52.061 --> 00:58:53.210
- Well we got to stand out.

00:58:53.210 --> 00:58:54.499
Evan, I've really enjoyed it.

00:58:54.499 --> 00:58:56.333
You keep standing out, everybody there.

00:58:56.333 --> 00:58:57.418
- Thank you guys for watching,

00:58:57.418 --> 00:58:59.386
we'll see you at the end of June.

00:58:59.386 --> 00:59:03.130
Continue to believe, and thank
you for tuning in to watch.

00:59:03.130 --> 00:59:04.354
- Great show, thanks!

00:59:04.354 --> 00:59:05.771
I love that, too.

